body:
This is done this way because static methods can also be called on instances, not just via `_callp_static`.
As for why not looking up both hash maps in `_callp`, my memory might be failing me, but I don't think the reason was related to performance or code simplicity. It was probably for compatibility reasons, which is something I was very meticulous about. Consider this example:

```cs
class Base : Node {
    static void Bar() { /* ... */ }
    void Foo() { /* ... */ }
}

class Derived : Base {
    void Bar() { /* ... */ }
    static void Foo() { /* ... */ }
}
```

Previously, this calling `Foo` or `Bar` on an instance `object.Call("Foo")` would have resolved to the methods declared in the `Derived` class. If we were to have to hash map lookups, one for instance methods and another one for static methods, that would result in either `Foo` or `Bar` (depending of which hash map you lookup first) resolving the methods in the `Base` class.

